//
// @FR-M-Unit — an arm's pattern names a VARIANT of the subject's enum.  An arm naming
// anything else can never be selected, and the compiler says so at the arm.
//
// The case this file exists for is the one that answered SILENTLY: a pattern name that
// resolves NOWHERE — a typo, or a variant renamed since the arm was written.  The
// diagnostic was gated on the name resolving to SOME definition, so `Grean` was refused
// when a struct of that name happened to exist and accepted when nothing did.  With a `_`
// in the match the dropped arm is invisible: the subject falls to the wildcard and the
// program answers the wildcard's value, on both backends, with nothing said.
//
// Deliberately NOT here: an arm whose name resolves to a non-variant definition (already
// refused with this same message), and a match whose subject enum is an unresolved
// cross-package forward reference (#375) — that arm is skipped on pass 1 by design and
// read on pass 2 with the enum resolved, which is why the test is on the SUBJECT being
// resolved and not on the pattern name resolving.
//
// @EXPECT_ERROR: 'Grean' is not a variant of Colour
// @EXPECT_ERROR: 'other' is not a variant of Colour
// @EXPECT_ERROR: 'zzz' is not a variant of Colour
//
// @falsified-at: a192aecbd (hand-measured) — every one of the three arms below compiled
//   there and ran to completion: `misspelled_with_a_wildcard(Green)` answered "other"
//   instead of "GREEN", `lowercase_name` answered 2 for `B`, and `covered_and_extra` ran
//   silent.  Zero diagnostics on either backend.

enum Colour { Red, Green, Blue }

// The renamed / misspelled variant, hidden by a `_`.  This is the shape that answers
// wrong rather than failing: `Green` reaches the wildcard.
fn misspelled_with_a_wildcard(c: Colour) -> text {
  return match c {
    Red => "red",
    Grean => "GREEN",
    _ => "other",
  };
}

// A lowercase name is not a pattern the grammar has (`pattern ::= '_' | 'null' | literal |
// range | CamelIdent [ '{' field_bind '}' ]`), so it is not a catch-all binding either —
// it is the same mistake wearing a different case.
fn lowercase_name(c: Colour) -> integer {
  return match c { Red => 1, other => 2, Green => 3, Blue => 4 };
}

// With every variant already covered, the extra arm is pure dead code — no exhaustiveness
// error follows it, so this arm is the whole of what the diagnostic has to catch.
fn covered_and_extra(c: Colour) -> integer {
  return match c { Red => 1, Green => 2, Blue => 3, zzz => 4 };
}

fn main() {
  println(misspelled_with_a_wildcard(Colour::Green));
  println("{lowercase_name(Colour::Green)}");
  println("{covered_and_extra(Colour::Blue)}");
}
